Lexable WCAG 2.2:

Cite only real WCAG 2.2 success criteria. Do not invent IDs or names.

Criteria commonly relevant in code review (not a full copy of WCAG):

- 1.1.1 Non-text Content — text alternatives for images, icons, SVG, and media.
- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships — programmatically determined structure (headings, lists, labels, tables).
- 1.3.5 Identify Input Purpose — autocomplete on common personal-data fields when collecting that data.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard — operable through a keyboard interface.
- 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap — focus must be movable away.
- 2.4.2 Page Titled — documents have a descriptive title.
- 2.4.3 Focus Order — meaningful sequence.
- 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context) — link purpose from text or context.
- 2.4.6 Headings and Labels — descriptive headings and labels.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible — visible focus indicator.
- 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum) — focused item is not entirely hidden.
- 2.5.3 Label in Name — accessible name contains the visible label text.
- 3.1.1 Language of Page — lang on the document.
- 3.3.1 Error Identification — errors identified in text.
- 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions — labels or instructions for input.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value — name, role, value, and changes for UI components.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages — status updates available to assistive technologies.

Static review can support detected issues for missing alt, missing labels, missing names, missing lang, and native-vs-ARIA markup.

Static review cannot pass: keyboard operability, keyboard traps, focus visibility, focus not obscured, contrast, target size, screen reader output, captions quality, or motion. Mark those runtime verification required, needs manual review, or not tested.

WCAG 2.2 retired 4.1.1. Do not cite 4.1.1.
